#{[17592186045463 #edb/ref 10 :activity.type/lesson #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045485 #edb/ref 10 :question.difficulty/easy #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045487 #edb/ref 10 :question.difficulty/moderate #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045492 #edb/ref 10 :answer-field.type/blank #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045493 #edb/ref 10 :answer-field.type/radio #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045498 #edb/ref 10 :answer.type/math #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1003 #edb/ref 17592186046659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045623 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045624 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045625 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045626 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045627 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045629 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045630 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045631 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045632 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045633 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045634 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045635 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045636 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045637 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046681 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046984 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1007 "Calculus II" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1008 "Our Calculus II course builds upon the differential calculus covered in Calculus I , providing in-depth coverage of integral calculus including integration techniques, applications of integration. Advanced topics such as series convergence tests, Taylor series, calculus with parametric and polar equations, and introductory differential equations are also covered. This comprehensive course will prepare students for further studies in advanced mathematics, engineering, statistics, machine learning, and other fields requiring a solid foundation in integral calculus." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1009 "Further your understanding of calculus: master advanced integration techniques, model real-world situations using differential equations, and more." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1012 106 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1013 "CA2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046645 #edb/ref 1014 #uuid "4965f9cc-fd21-5d50-b441-6e80983b17dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048568 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186051054 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048568 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186175968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048568 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186175969 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048568 #edb/ref 1031 "The Shell Method: Rotating a Region Between Two Curves About the X-Axis" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048568 #edb/ref 1033 3641 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048568 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "aa7fefd1-2912-58e4-b2a0-bbf07ec60fc5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050159 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050159 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186068815 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050159 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186068816 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050159 #edb/ref 1031 "The Shell Method: Rotating a Region About the X-Axis" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050159 #edb/ref 1033 415 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050159 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "32cf9e59-1dd8-51df-b1af-51ac52d78285" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051054 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186096828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051054 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186096829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051054 #edb/ref 1031 "The Shell Method: Rotation About the Y-Axis" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051054 #edb/ref 1033 1084 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051054 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "7d6f9e38-bd31-5a75-ae9b-e0749e0230ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186174676 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539541 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186174676 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539575 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186174676 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539748 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186174676 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539804 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186174676 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186174676 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186174676 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186174676 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186539748 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186174676 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ab089e7c-7f52-5582-ac6f-752c4f8bf60f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175818 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8855" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175818 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the finite region bounded by the curves $x=3y-y^2$ and $x=y,$  as shown below. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/7e/7eca2a7eecfb9555a7e1555555a6d7b435a794e5715537d4fe94998ef00d124a.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175818 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7520ab43-c5b9-541b-80a9-4af1b91b2428" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175818 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let $f(y)=3y-y^2$ and $g(y)=y.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ 3y-{y}^{2} & =y \\\\ 2y-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ y(2-y) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions corresponding to our region are $y=0$ and $y=2.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=2.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](images/86/861a1300845e3832a201dd00410d462350f33a4654332b6b6fa7140dc6476dbb.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=g(y)=y,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=f(y)=3y-y^2.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([\\text{right}]-[\\text{left}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y([3y-{y}^{2}]-[y])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y(2y-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(2{y}^{2}-{y}^{3})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi (2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{3}\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =2\\pi ({2\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{2}-{\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}|}_{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\pi (2(\\frac{8}{3}-0)-(\\frac{16}{4}-0)) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{16}{3}-4) \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175819 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8857" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175819 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the finite region bounded by the curves $x=2-y^2,$ $x=y,$ and the $x$-axis, as shown below. Using the shell method, find an integral expression for the volume generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/16/16a30b8310128aa588b3278b5a0984193ce57cbb5ea9118fc167a636487363b8.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175819 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9f7f3606-5c49-51d0-b4e5-cffd0310b6c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175819 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let $f(y)=2-y^2$ and $g(y)=y.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ 2-{y}^{2} & =y \\\\ {y}^{2}+y-2 & =0 \\\\ (y+2)(y-1) & =0 \\\\ y & =-2,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution corresponding to our region is $y=1.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=1.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](images/f6/f69e2c1cd3b099a4ba192357db32274867c4f296e2c34e31e5110ee31b270317.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=g(y)=y,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=f(y)=2-y^2.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([\\text{right}]-[\\text{left}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y([2-{y}^{2}]-[y])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y(2-y-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175968 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186176404 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175968 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186176406 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175968 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540025 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175968 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540031 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175968 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540103 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175968 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540106 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175968 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540107 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175968 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Volume of a Solid Using the Shell Method" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175968 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186175818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175968 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "2737d1a7-2af9-54ed-8c42-5191fc68a31b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175969 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050159 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175969 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186176215 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175969 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186176403 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175969 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175969 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540053 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175969 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540083 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175969 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540084 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175969 #edb/ref 1039 "Constructing an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175969 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186175819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175969 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "3cd62b9c-0fbc-5a1e-b923-b2be8a55e44a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176158 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176702 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176158 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176703 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176158 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176158 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176158 #edb/ref 1045 "The Shell Method: Rotating a Region Between Two Curves About the X-Axis" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176158 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186176702 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176158 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176158 #edb/ref 1050 660.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176158 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "d4bf6855-3ee9-564c-a98f-cca62f229eef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176403 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186174676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176403 #edb/ref 1069 "q-17023" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176403 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176403 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/a5/a5a3a246f2ecb4bee6cfc46f34d14b5abc9fcf22ed0d6173ed465bfab2cfeca6.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curves $x=5y-2{y}^{2}$ and $x=y,$ as shown above. Find an integral expression for the volume generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176403 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2289657c-a9c3-56eb-97a8-f3d9729a0b73" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176403 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let $f(y)=5y-2{y}^{2}$ and $g(y)=y.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ 5y-2{y}^{2} & =y \\\\ 2{y}^{2}-4y & =0 \\\\ 2y(y-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $y=0$ and $y=2.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=2.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](images/d8/d873c0acb12392e4ea40f761c72c1903a91c4f0e9fef04b586a71697957e2401.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=g(y)=y,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=f(y)=5y-2{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([\\text{right}]-[\\text{left}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y([5y-2{y}^{2}]-[y])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y(4y-2{y}^{2})\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176702 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176702 #edb/ref 1061 33079 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176702 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0e02ebde-56c8-5084-9591-4df36f3cd368" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176702 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176983 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176703 #edb/ref 1061 33080 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176703 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "06aa8368-cb63-543a-ad96-6e6d78f9e484" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176703 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186175968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176704 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176703 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176704 #edb/ref 1061 33083 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176704 #edb/ref 1062 #uu