54 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "4ba66103-f7c3-5909-8944-c611bc72d320" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539973 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539656 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539973 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539973 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539973 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186539656 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539973 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "9c63fd60-a918-5151-ad1a-213063aeef6f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539987 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539674 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539987 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539987 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539987 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186539674 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539987 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b70140cd-8dd2-5535-a3c9-379587f1fc13" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539988 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539518 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539988 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539569 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539988 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539665 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539988 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539758 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539988 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539988 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539988 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539988 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186539665 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539988 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b7ab163e-5d5d-5698-b7ab-03eea7c74f9e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540012 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539509 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540012 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539533 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540012 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539592 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540012 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539782 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540012 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539837 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540012 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540012 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540012 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186539533 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540012 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "eefd7b42-cc63-5201-b954-24eada1a35de" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540025 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540025 #edb/ref 1069 "q-107142" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540025 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540025 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/92/920aca572a3b6fc828d6c37246b348aa2b017d1bc4e022ea06c12d0fa09ad6ee.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curves $x=(1-y)^{2}$ and $y=\\sqrt{1-x},$ as shown above. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540025 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1404f19f-22e0-51b4-954a-73312901eafa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540025 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write the second function in terms $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{1-x} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1-x \\\\ x & =1-{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let $f(y)=1-{y}^{2}$ and $g(y)=(1-y)^{2}.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ 1-{y}^{2} & =(1-y)^{2} \\\\ 1-{y}^{2} & =1-2y+{y}^{2} \\\\ 2{y}^{2}-2y & =0 \\\\ 2y(y-1) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=1.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](images/02/02e6b4204b1edf682dd332e28830dc84aa0e41d3728c66442d777f6fe9daccfc.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=g(y)=(1-y)^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=f(y)=1-{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([\\text{right}]-[\\text{left}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y([1-{y}^{2}]-[(1-y)^{2}])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y([1-{y}^{2}]-[1-2y+{y}^{2}])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y(2y-2{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{y}^{2}-2{y}^{3})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4\\pi ({\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{3}\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =4\\pi ({\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{1}-{\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}|}_{0}^{1}) \\\\  & =4\\pi ((\\frac{1}{3}-0)-(\\frac{1}{4}-0)) \\\\  & =4\\pi (\\frac{1}{12}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540031 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539947 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540031 #edb/ref 1069 "q-107304" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540031 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540031 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/5e/5eac1b1d21378d64b90fc2463eb7664b30224594ba5d63aa70a7c412097012a0.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curves $x=1+y$ and $x=1-2y+{y}^{2},$ as shown above. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540031 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1e2fc7b4-11f3-5f1c-807e-b40bd8df74a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540031 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let $f(y)=1+y$ and $g(y)=1-2y+{y}^{2}.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ 1+y & =1-2y+{y}^{2} \\\\ 3y-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ y(3-y) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions corresponding to our region are $y=0$ and $y=3.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=3.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](images/84/84e62b99598649ac48d55171b0d6bf7e5d26e38a6ad94c46ef9c89145e60e29a.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=g(y)=1-2y+{y}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=f(y)=1+y.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([\\text{right}]-[\\text{left}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}y([1+y]-[1-2y+{y}^{2}])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}y(3y-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}(3{y}^{2}-{y}^{3})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi (3{\\int }_{0}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{3}{y}^{3}\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =2\\pi ({3\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{3}-{\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}|}_{0}^{3}) \\\\  & =2\\pi (3(\\frac{27}{3}-0)-(\\frac{81}{4}-0)) \\\\  & =2\\pi (27-\\frac{81}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{27\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540032 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539952 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540032 #edb/ref 1069 "q-107218" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540032 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540032 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/89/8957a7db313379597a21e6b51d6c4493058af207a2d8edd5fafb8e549ba45aae.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curves $x=1+y$ and $x=1+{y}^{3},$ as shown above. Find an integral expression for the volume generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540032 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1ede84af-72eb-557a-81fe-42dab11d2478" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540032 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let $f(y)=1+y$ and $g(y)=1+{y}^{3}.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ 1+y & =1+{y}^{3} \\\\ y-{y}^{3} & =0 \\\\ y(1-{y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ y(1-y)(1+y) & =0 \\\\ y & =-1,0,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions corresponding to our region are $y=0$ and $y=1.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=1.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](images/b9/b91dedd1282ce1e9d8546bc6f7c52eba8741a2cd49613a03ef62866a9ba91b0c.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=g(y)=1+{y}^{3},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=f(y)=1+y.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([\\text{right}]-[\\text{left}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y([1+y]-[1+{y}^{3}])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y(y-{y}^{3})\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540053 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539988 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540053 #edb/ref 1069 "q-126191" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540053 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540053 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/da/dadb1e7740ee89fbbf9cffe69501ebd7be655ec9aaee30071b09fc767e80092c.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curves $x=1+2y-{y}^{2}$ and $x=2y,$ as shown above. Find an integral expression for the volume generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540053 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5dabe85e-85d9-5f83-8489-ec7933e9dac9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540053 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let $f(y)=1+2y-{y}^{2}$ and $g(y)=2y.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ 1+2y-{y}^{2} & =2y \\\\ 1-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (1-y)(1+y) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution corresponding to our region is $y=1.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=1.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](images/d9/d9f7cf87c59b7a0fc9600178795524f35a72b12466385e63eafa35c1fdb3537c.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=g(y)=2y,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=f(y)=1+2y-{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([\\text{right}]-[\\text{left}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y([1+2y-{y}^{2}]-[2y])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y(1+2y-{y}^{2}-2y)\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y(1-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540083 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540083 #edb/ref 1069 "q-126325" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540083 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540083 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/b6/b61c628a012f6bde3144254b521e11f8803e089cd7f83f2c19a1255cf22c0e3a.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curves $x=\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}},$ $x=-\\frac{1}{2}y+1,$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above. Find an integral expression for the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540083 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b0ffc976-87a0-51e6-8ff5-4a0a8b70546a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540083 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let $f(y)=\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}$ and $g(y)=-\\frac{1}{2}y+1.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ \\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}} & =-\\frac{1}{2}y+1 \\\\ 4-{y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{2}-y+1 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{4}{y}^{2}+y+3 & =0 \\\\ 5{y}^{2}-4y-12 & =0 \\\\ (5y+6)(y-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution corresponding to our region is $y=2.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=2.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](images/12/122a21e72dc7473cc34ed3ece0325294eadcc86cbe8a5b91e4dfe4be77b369c0.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=g(y)=-\\frac{1}{2}y+1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=f(y)=\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([\\text{right}]-[\\text{left}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y([\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}]-[-\\frac{1}{2}y+1])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y(\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2}y-1)\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540084 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539954 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540084 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106122" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540084 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540084 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/30/30aa4c5e72919d09a0a2323dc8af90a73810047857f3c89fe9c3005a10c195ae.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curves $x=4y-{y}^{2}$ and $x=2y,$ as shown above. Find an integral expression for the volume generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540084 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b61e8b5a-f6d1-5f01-ad3a-3725586d9405" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540084 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let $f(y)=4y-{y}^{2}$ and $g(y)=2y.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ 4y-{y}^{2} & =2y \\\\ 2y-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ y(2-y) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions corresponding to our region are $y=0$ and $y=2.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=2.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](images/cb/cbfd6c409590e6b6d350b5ae8f6531944467e90991b17582febe228a6a8dfdb2.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=g(y)=2y,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=f(y)=4y-{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([\\text{right}]-[\\text{left}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y([4y-{y}^{2}]-[2y])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y(2y-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540103 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539923 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540103 #edb/ref 1069 "q-126377" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540103 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540103 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/48/482781bb21d011df5a9140b2b1fb0ebe1108f746c0055077d4f4e9a91fce735d.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curves $y=\\sqrt{2-x},$ $x={y}^{2},$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540103 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e40037f0-3304-53a5-a4f4-7220f338664f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540103 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write the second function in terms $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{2-x} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =2-x \\\\ x & =2-{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let $f(y)={y}^{2}$ and $g(y)=2-{y}^{2}.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ {y}^{2} & =2-{y}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ y & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution corresponding to our region is $y=1.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=1.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](images/48/48905a174d688fdb3e099debb7840173a2ce047816003a5be9c089997196f9fb.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=f(y)={y}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=g(y)=2-{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([\\text{right}]-[\\text{left}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y([2-{y}^{2}]-[{y}^{2}])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y(2-2{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(y-{y}^{3})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4\\pi ({\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{3}\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =4\\pi ({\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}|}_{0}^{1}-{\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}|}_{0}^{1}) \\\\  & =4\\pi ((\\frac{1}{2}-0)-(\\frac{1}{4}-0)) \\\\  & =4\\pi (\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540106 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539987 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540106 #edb/ref 1069 "q-352199" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540106 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540106 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/1d/1d8d3dfa1134b7fd5861ff70e493ead2227dd2d322e8436e28a903e0c596a408.png)\n\nConsider the region bounded by the curves $x=4y-{y}^{2},$ $y=\\sqrt{x},$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above. Calculate the volume $V$ of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540106 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "efbcafae-ea63-52c2-855e-a9cfa22be6a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540106 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write the second function in terms of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{x} \\\\ x & ={y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let $f(y)=4y-{y}^{2}$ and $g(y)={y}^{2}.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ 4y-{y}^{2} & ={y}^{2} \\\\ 4y-2{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2y(2-y) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution corresponding to our region is $y=2.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=2.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](images/c5/c5a117e2af85e3380942939812442eac7b899a2259af79ad032654fc5662a767.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=g(y)={y}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=f(y)=4y-{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([\\text{right}]-[\\text{left}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y([4y-{y}^{2}]-[{y}^{2}])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y(4y-2{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(4{y}^{2}-2{y}^{3})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi (4{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{3}\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =2\\pi ({4\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{2}-{2\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}|}_{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\pi (4(\\frac{8}{3}-0)-2(\\frac{16}{4}-0)) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{32}{3}-8) \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540107 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539973 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540107 #edb/ref 1069 "q-352372" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540107 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540107 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/dc/dcb67e2d3fe9a413c02bb00b6d8cf7c0ac8825db1d360ab883f08baa3b6c2e9c.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curves $x=7-y-{y}^{2},$ $x=5-{y}^{2},$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above. Calculate the volume $V$ of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540107 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f03e4c2d-a256-544b-8a7f-59a90059d73b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540107 #edb/ref 1074 "Now, let $f(y)=7-y-{y}^{2}$ and $g(y)=5-{y}^{2}.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ 7-y-{y}^{2} & =5-{y}^{2} \\\\ 2-y & =0 \\\\ y & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution corresponding to our region is $y=2.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=2.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](images/cf/cfb914bf7ed0a0c6b9f784d46a6761cd97c352fcc4e82abb29a72dc12d8ab7ca.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=g(y)=5-{y}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=f(y)=7-y-{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([\\text{right}]-[\\text{left}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y([7-y-{y}^{2}]-[5-{y}^{2}])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y(2-y)\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(2y-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi (2{\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =2\\pi ({2\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}|}_{0}^{2}-{\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\pi (2(\\frac{4}{2}-0)-(\\frac{8}{3}-0)) \\\\  & =2\\pi (4-\\frac{8}{3}) \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
